{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1396207-let-s-talk-about-bleed-and-how-do-you-handle-it","id":1396207,"title":"Let's talk about \"Bleed\" and how do you handle it?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-13T13:46:54-07:00","id":1396207,"title":"Let's talk about \"Bleed\" and how do you handle it?","content":"Bleed here being &quot;<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Bleed is experienced by a player when her thoughts and feelings are influenced by those of her character, or vice versa.&quot;</span></span></span><br /> As per <a href=\"https://nordiclarp.org/wiki/Bleed#:~:text=Bleed%20is%20when%20emotions%20bleeds,her%20character%2C%20or%20vice%20versa.\" target=\"_blank\">https://nordiclarp.org/wiki/Bleed#:~...0vice%20versa.</a><br /> <br /> So Bleed... this is something I encountered recently when playing VTM for the first time. And it made me wonder how does people handle bleed?<br /> <br /> Before VTM, I have been playing TRPG for over a decade. Mainly D&amp;D and Pathfinder. And while I may find myself attached to some characters, there never was an overly deep emotional attachment. I just never encountered bleed while playing those TRPGs. There may be times that you feel frustrated or use a character to vent emotions, but never to the strong emotional bleed I got in VTM.<br /> May be it has to do with the fact that those TRPGs there is a strong game element to them. Also playing heroic characters, with proper backstories (if you want) and a fantasy setting, may have made it easier to feel detached from them.<br /> <br /> Playing VTM gave me very weird strong emotions that confused me for a while, before I realise it was bleed that I was getting, specifically bleed-out. My character was affecting me, emotionally.<br /> It made me upset at certain people in real-life(RL) at one point and totally crushed me at another moment.<br /> <br /> For context on the part that crashed me, we are playing VTM darkage play by post. My character had a deep bond with his ghouls in-game. And when a certain historical event caused the deaths of a number of his ghouls, my character was devastated. AND it made me felt really bad in RL... like I was totally upset emotionally. It felt like family members, friend or beloved pets had actually died.<br /> <br /> It was at this point that I read up on bleed and realise what I was going through. It caught me totally off guard. Like how in the world am I so emotionally invested in characters that are not even &quot;real&quot;. I was feeling like I was the character.<br /> I had to actually get two fellow players (with their characters) to RP out my character's grief in a scene, to help bleed off (?) the feelings I'm getting from the bleed-out.<br /> <br /> As I dived deeper into understanding bleed and come to kinda understand it better, I now made an effort to &quot;isolate&quot; my character's ego from mine. Using my character's name and 3rd person, instead of words like I or me.<br /> <br /> So has anyone else encountered bleed before? How do you cope with it? Is it something more common in VTM compared to other TRPGs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26067,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26067-wingstorm","user":"Wingstorm","date":". 06-13-2020, 04:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26067-wingstorm","name":"Wingstorm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-13T15:26:40-07:00","id":1396222,"title":"","content":"it was werewolf, but i've had a player make some very self destructive moves the character probably wouldn't have. i dont remember details, but the sept gave her a temporary week or month long exile for her to think over her failures. Issue is that took us five minutes to go over out of character, so the character came back to the sept quite distraught and immediately got into more trouble. sonething that wouldn't have happened had the player been given time to get into the right mindset. I had us do a redo. a lesson worth sharing i think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-13T17:24:19-07:00","id":1396252,"title":"","content":"Not personally, I've been fairly good about separating myself and my characters. I did really regret that the way I played my first Vampire character (scary as hell) seemed to have a strong out-of-game influence on a fellow player when his character came into conflict with mine, and he abandoned a nuanced and interesting social character for a two-dimensional combat monster in its place. If I had that to do over again I'd have worked against his character in less overwhelming ways to keep him engaged with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-13T20:59:50-07:00","id":1396304,"title":"","content":"I'm mostly ST, so this might not affect me that much because of my role. But when I play I noticed that I tend to be affected less when playing RPG systems that feel pretty 'artificial' for me. For example Call of Cthulhu's BRP-System felt very natural to me and therefore bleed effects might have been stronger. VTM always felt more gamey for me, because I was thinking in dice pools and was planning XP-usage and blood economy. And I'm not really a power gamer or even just an 'effective' gamer. I guess what I'd like to say is: One of the best ways to create room between you and your character is to think about a certain situation not from an in-world-perspective but from a player's (or a game mechanical) perspective. The same might be true if one thinks instead from a story perspective (&quot;My ghouls just died. my character might be devasted, but it could be a chance for a really dramatic plot twist.&quot;), because I never had noticable bleed effects occur to me or my players while playing FATE or other story-centric RPGs.<br /> <br /> I hope my perspective was clearly-phrased and maybe a little bit helpful. Best wishes to you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">&quot;Life's barely long enough to get good at one thing. So be careful what you get good at.&quot;</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26196-saturno","name":"Saturno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-13T23:32:35-07:00","id":1396319,"title":"","content":"This was way more common in my games when I was much younger. I don't think I've really run across any instances of it (in me or others) since I hit 22, except for with my occasional teenage players.<br /> <br /> If I ran into it in my games nowadays, I'd see it as a sign that it was time to stop playing for a while.<br /> <br /> Your mileage may vary, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13136-legendre","name":"Legendre","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"328"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-14T18:25:21-07:00","id":1396571,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26067-wingstorm\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26067\" data-vbnamecard=\"26067\">Wingstorm</a> <br /> <br /> What you've experienced is common. In order to enjoy a ttrpg it is necessary to have some amount of player buy-in. That is to say, the game is more entertaining if the players care about what is happening inside the story.<br /> <br /> This is true not just of ttrpgs.<br /> <br /> There is a whole <a href=\"https://www.cosmopolitan.com/entertainment/celebs/news/g3144/tearjerkers-movies/?slide=32\" target=\"_blank\">genre of media</a> designed to elicit the response you describe. Tearjerkers are hugely popular, and I'm not ashamed to say I love them. Fine, yes, the film is dated and problematic, but to this day I cannot watch <a href=\"https://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/1010417_imitation_of_life\" target=\"_blank\">Imitation of Life</a> without ugly sobbing. (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_DXIaPDwog\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;Momma! Momma! I didn't mean it, momma!&quot;</a>... oh, great here come the tears again.)<br /> <br /> All stories rely on audience buy-in to create emotional involvement, from <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nhWgTSQdRmo\" target=\"_blank\">Shakespeare</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1VM2eLhvsSM\" target=\"_blank\">tv commercials for sugary brown water</a>. Hit songs have been written about how easily a listener's <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pdz5kCaCRFM\" target=\"_blank\">emotions can be manipulated</a>. We can all remember times when the ending of a television series, death of a favorite character, or especially intense film elicited reactions comparable to what one would expect from the death of a friend or relative.<br /> <br /> As with all good things, however, just the right amount can become too much.<br /> <br /> Often, the most empathetic players have greater buy-in, and those who are more aloof have less. A good ST will observe their players, and note their reactions to the story. It's important for STs to actively elicit players to describe their experience - are things too intense or not intense enough? For their part, players must take time to give honest feedback, and if the ST doesn't ask, the player should mention if the intensity is at the wrong level for them.<br /> <br /> If the players are too detached, there need to be more story elements which players can easily identify with.<br /> <br /> If the players are too involved, it can be helpful for the ST to insert quick changes of tone to keep things distant enough to be enjoyable. That is, in a heavy drama with lots of death and despair, throw in some comedy, intellectual discussion, or a light-hearted side-quest. It's important to bring players into the story, but it's also important to lead them back out again. Falling action is a vital part of any story, even an episodic story. It puts the story in its proper perspective as a fun thing to do, rather than a thing that takes over our lives.<br /> <br /> If this doesn't fit easily in the story, introduce such a tone shift out of character. Maybe, after a particularly scary or depressing session, play some happy music and have some snacks. Try changing the physical environment subtly, with lighting or background music, when the game session has begun and ended. This trains players to differentiate the start and end of the session, helping them to leave the drama at the table.<br /> <br /> As a personal aside, it can be important to remain aware of why such buy-in has become problematic. I have found, personally, that times when I find myself over involved in a game, it has helped to check in with an outside observer, like a trusted acquaintance or a mental health professional, to see what other issues might be pushing me into such an over-wrought state. Usually, if I deal with those issues, and give myself a Monty Python marathon my mood bounces right back.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-14T19:09:13-07:00","id":1396576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1396207-let-s-talk-about-bleed-and-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1396571#post1396571\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What you've experienced is common. In order to enjoy a ttrpg it is necessary to have some amount of player buy-in. That is to say, the game is more entertaining if the players care about what is happening inside the story.<br /> <br /> This is true not just of ttrpgs</div> </div> </div> </div>This is my take. Media <i>should </i>have some kind of emotional resonance in the viewer, if it doesn't, there won't be engagement.<br /> <br /> Bleed, per se, is not a bad thing. Players <i>should </i>be empathising with what their characters are going through. It's only an issue when it gets out of hand and there are real-world disputes as a result of it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26181-malksam","name":"MalkSam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26181_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}